flcomp -- the FreeLFS Wayland compositor
========================================

flcomp is the display server of the FreeLFS desktop. It talks to the
kernel directly (DRM/KMS dumb buffers, libseat for device access, libinput
for mice and keyboards) and composites on the CPU, so it needs no GPU
driver and about 20 MB of RAM at 1080p. No wlroots. GPU clients (the browser)
send dmabuf buffers, which are read into the same CPU copy (see "dmabuf").

Starting
  flcomp               the session: starts flpanel (restarted if it dies,
                       up to 5 times) and runs until Ctrl-Alt-Backspace or
                       "Log out" in the panel; exit status 0 then
  flcomp --firstboot   runs flsetup alone and exits with its status
  /etc/profile.d/desktop.sh starts flcomp for non-root users on tty1, with
  stderr in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flcomp.log. It does not exec: when flcomp ends
  with a non-zero status (a crash signal is 128 + number) it runs flcrash, the
  "Something went wrong" screen; Enter there starts flcomp again, Esc leaves to
  a shell. Needs seatd running and your user in its group.

Keys
  Super (tapped)       tells the panel to toggle the start menu
  XF86AudioRaiseVolume, LowerVolume, Mute
                       swallowed and sent to the panel (fl_shell volume_key); without a
                       panel flcomp runs wpctl itself; raise and lower repeat while held
  XF86MonBrightnessUp, Down
                       backlight in 5 % steps, at least 5 % (media.c), brightness= in
                       desktop.conf; XF86AudioMicMute toggles the microphone
                       Every one of them shows the on-screen indicator (osd.c)
  Alt-Tab, Alt-Shift-Tab
                       cycle windows forwards and backwards (hold Alt); a row of window
                       pictures (switcher.c) shows the choice, the picked one in accent
  Super+Left, Super+Right   snap the window to the left or right half (the other key undoes it)
  Super+Up             maximize;  Super+Down  restore, or minimize
  Super+E              flfiles;  Super+T or Ctrl-Alt-T  flterm
  Super+L              locks the screen at once and starts fllock (if /usr/bin/fllock exists), see "Lock screen"
  Print                the whole screen: Snappel (snappel --full: saved to ~/pictures/screenshots, with a
                       notification) when it is installed, else flcomp saves it itself to
                       ~/pictures/screenshot-YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS.png (shot.c, libpng) and shows the indicator
  Shift+Print          snappel --region;  Super+Shift+S  the Snappel window ($FLCOMP_SNAPPEL names another program)
  Super+Ctrl+arrows    switch workspace (along the grid, no wrap)
  Super+Shift+arrows   move the focused window (and its dialogs) to the next workspace; the view follows
  Super+Tab            the workspace overview (see "Workspaces"); Tab, arrows, 1-9, Enter, Esc inside
  Ctrl-Shift-Esc       General Manager (the task manager: processes, performance, startup apps)
  XF86AudioPlay/Stop/Next/Prev   `flmusic --ctl play-pause|stop|next|previous` (MPRIS; volume, mute,
                       brightness and microphone keys are described in media.c)
  Ctrl-Alt-Backspace   quit the session
  Ctrl-Alt-F1..F12     switch virtual terminal
  make check-keys      headless test of all of the above
  make check-ws        headless test of workspaces, the overview, fl_shell 5, fl_screencopy and the Snappel keys

Windows draw their own title bars. flcomp handles stacking, click to focus,
move and resize (xdg_toplevel.move/resize), maximize, fullscreen, minimize,
popups, clipboard (wl_data_device). Drag and drop, touch and server-side
decorations are not implemented.

dmabuf (dmabuf.c, gl.c): zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 version 3, single-plane ARGB8888,
XRGB8888, ABGR8888, XBGR8888. A dmabuf is read when the client commits and
released at once, like a wl_shm buffer; compositing stays on the CPU. Linear
buffers are mmap'ed and copied (this is all there is on llvmpipe, a KMS-only
device or without a GPU: only the LINEAR modifier is announced). On a real
GPU the first client that binds the global makes flcomp start EGL on the DRM
device through GBM; then every modifier the GPU can sample is announced and a
tiled or compressed buffer is imported as an EGLImage, drawn by GLES2 into a
linear texture and read back into the surface copy. A session of shm clients
never loads EGL: gl.c reaches EGL, GBM and GLES through dlopen when the first client
binds the global (linked, they pulled libgallium, libLLVM and SPIRV-Tools into every
session: flcomp went from 33 MB to 12 MB PSS on the idle live desktop). FLCOMP_NO_GL=1 turns the GPU path off. wl_drm (old EGL
clients without dmabuf) and a GPU compositing path are not implemented.

Keyboard layout comes from /etc/keyboard.conf (LAYOUT=de, optional VARIANT=
and OPTIONS=, default us) and can be changed at run time with
fl_shell.set_keyboard_layout (not saved).

Globals: wl_compositor 4, wl_subcompositor 1, wl_shm, wl_output 3, wl_seat 5, xwayland_shell_v1 1 (Xwayland only),
wl_data_device_manager 3, xdg_wm_base 3, zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 3, fl_shell 9.

desktop.conf (config.c, ~/.config/freelfs/desktop.conf, $FLUI_CONF; watched with
inotify, changes apply at once, except display mode and scale)
  pointer_speed=-100..100   pointer_accel=adaptive|flat   left_handed=0|1
  mouse_natural=0|1   touchpad_natural=0|1   tap_click=0|1   typing_disable=0|1
      libinput settings on every device, present and hot-plugged; a touchpad is a
      pointer with tap fingers, the other pointers are mice
  key_repeat_delay=ms (600)   key_repeat_rate=per second (25)   numlock=0|1
      wl_keyboard.repeat_info to every client; Num Lock is switched on (and the
      keyboard LEDs follow the xkb state) when the setting turns on
  night_light=0|1   night_warmth=0..100
      colour tables applied while copying to the screen (green and blue go down)
  brightness=percent   written to the backlight's sysfs file at start
  mode=1920x1080@60   scale=100..200   read at start
  theme=dark|light  accent=...   the colour scheme of everything flcomp draws itself (theme.c, flpalette.h from libflui)
  animations=on|reduced|off  animation_speed=0.1..4   see "Animations"
  panel_position=bottom|top  panel_height=36..64   where the panel sits and how much room windows get
  titlebar=normal|compact  buttons=right|left   the frame of X windows (Wayland windows draw their own, libflui follows the same keys)
  power_saver=0|1   frame cap and dimmed backlight (see "Power saving")
  performance=auto|on|off   see "Performance mode" (default auto)
  lock_on_lid=0|1   closing the lid locks the screen (default 1, see "Lock screen")
Display (output.c, drm.c): the screen has a mode; the desktop is laid out at
mode / scale (at least 640 x 400) and enlarged by the backend when it copies to
the screen: pixel doubling at 200 %, bilinear in between (a little soft). The
pointer's motion is scaled to match. wl_output reports the desktop's size.
fl_shell set_output(w, h, hz, scale) tries a new mode and scale: the old one
comes back after 15 seconds unless confirm_output arrives (or the asking client
dies); revert_output goes back at once. The panel is restarted (it sizes itself
once at start), maximized windows are refitted, windows off screen come back.
Events output_mode (every mode, flag 1 current, 2 preferred) and output_state.

fl_shell (protocol/fl-shell.xml, installed to /usr/share/flcomp)
  requests: set_panel(surface, height), set_menu(surface), activate(id),
            minimize(id), close(id), set_keyboard_layout(name), logout,
            destroy; since 2: set_flyout(surface, x); since 3: set_output(w, h,
            hz, scale), confirm_output, revert_output, show_osd(kind, percent, flags);
            since 4: set_notifications(surface), lock(surface), unlock; since 7: set_window_entry(id, x, y, w, h)
  events:   window_added(id, app_id, title), window_changed(id, title,
            focused, minimized), window_removed(id), menu_dismissed,
            menu_toggle, keyboard_layout(name); since 2: flyout_dismissed,
            volume_key(action: 0 raise, 1 lower, 2 mute); since 3: output_mode(w,
            h, hz, flags), output_state(scale, w, h, pending), output_failed; since 4:
            lock_state(locked)
The panel surface is placed at the bottom edge (the top edge with panel_position=top in desktop.conf: the menu and
flyouts then open below it, windows get the room under it) and should be as wide as the
output (wl_output.mode). The menu is shown above the left edge of the panel
while it has a buffer; a click outside menu and panel hides it and sends
menu_dismissed (attach a buffer again to show it). A click on the panel
itself never dismisses it, the panel decides. The flyout (set_flyout) works the
same way for a small popup directly above the panel, centred on x and kept on
screen; it sends flyout_dismissed.

Desktop icons (fl_shell 6 set_desktop(surface, x, y); surface.c, render.c): a small transparent surface
drawn above the wallpaper and below every window, on every workspace, at x, y. Only what is drawn takes the
pointer. flpanel (deskicons.c) uses it for the programs whose .desktop file says X-FreeLFS-Desktop-Icon=true
("FreeLFS install" on the live system); the compositor has no icon logic of its own.

Notifications (fl_shell 4 set_notifications): the panel's card surface goes to the top
right of the screen, 16 px from the edges, above windows, panel, menu and flyout. It is
transparent: a pixel with alpha 0 takes no pointer input, so the gaps between the cards
click through. It never has keyboard focus and is not shown while the screen is locked.

Workspaces (xdg.c, overview.c; fl_shell 5; desktop.conf workspaces=COLSxROWS, default 2x2, 1..8 each)
  A grid of virtual desktops, openSUSE/KDE style. A window is on exactly one (window.ws); only the
  windows of the workspace in view are drawn, picked, focused, in Alt-Tab and in list_windows. New
  windows open where you are, dialogs on their parent's workspace. Switching focuses the newest
  window of the new workspace; activating a window from the panel goes to its workspace. Changing
  workspaces=... applies live: windows on removed workspaces move to the last one.
  Overview (Super+Tab or fl_shell toggle_overview): every workspace as a tile in the grid's shape with
  its windows as pictures, drawn over the work area and under the panel; click a tile to go there, a
  window to go there and focus it, a click beside the tiles or Esc puts it away.
  fl_shell version 5 (older bindings work unchanged): events workspaces(cols, rows, current) and
  window_workspace(id, index); requests set_workspace, move_window, toggle_overview. The panel's
  pager (src/flpanel) uses them.

Screenshots (screencopy.c; protocol/fl-screencopy.xml, global fl_screencopy_manager 1)
  capture_output, capture_region, capture_window and list_windows give a client the desktop as flcomp
  draws it (no pointer, Alt-Tab row or indicator, and without the asking process's own windows, found
  by pid), or one window alone from its own pixels, into a wl_shm buffer (frame: buffer, copy, ready or
  failed). Everything fails while the screen is locked. Snappel (src/snappel) is the client.

Lock screen (lock.c; fl_shell 4 lock/unlock, Super+L, the lid)
  The lock is the compositor's, not the lock program's. While locked nothing but the lock
  client's surface is drawn (0,0, meant to cover the output), picked or given the keyboard;
  there is no panel, no window, no notification, no menu, no Alt-Tab, no Print and no
  Ctrl-Alt-Backspace, and key bindings do nothing except Ctrl-Alt-F1..F12 (virtual
  terminals) and the media keys (volume, brightness). The cursor is still drawn.
  fl_shell lock(surface) locks (it works whether Super+L did it first or the client starts on
  its own, as from the panel's Lock button) and only that client's unlock ends it. A second
  lock request from another client while one holds it is the "locked" protocol error.
  Every version-4 binding gets lock_state(1) and lock_state(0).
  If the lock client dies the screen stays locked and shows a solid colour. flcomp starts
  the lock program again after 300 ms (FLCOMP_LOCK=CMD, default fllock; up to three quick
  deaths in a row, 5 s or less each); if nothing shows the lock within about two seconds
  a hint appears (text from the built-in font of lockfont.h, which tools/genlockfont.c
  makes from IBM Plex Sans, because flcomp has no fonts and must not depend on the
  program that failed): "Screen locked. The lock program is not running. Press Enter to
  start it again." Enter starts it again; Ctrl-Alt-F2 opens a console.
  Super+L only locks where there is a lock program (/usr/bin/fllock, or FLCOMP_LOCK).
  The lid switch (libinput) locks the same way, unless lock_on_lid=0 in desktop.conf.
  There is no suspend path in the system yet (flpower knows off and restart); whatever
  later suspends the machine should run fllock first, or just let the lid do it.

Animations (anim.c; desktop.conf animations=on|reduced|off, animation_speed=FACTOR)
  Short, flat, eased motion, no wobble and no bounce, 100 to 200 ms (divided by the speed factor):
  window open (fade in, 96 % to 100 %) and close (a picture of the last frame fades out), minimize and restore
  (the window shrinks towards its panel entry and comes back from it; the panel tells flcomp where the entry is with
  fl_shell 7 set_window_entry), maximize, snap and restore (the geometry moves from the old place to the new one),
  the workspace switch (windows slide along the move), the overview (it zooms from 108 % while fading in and out) and
  the start menu, flyouts and notification cards (a fade with a short slide from the panel edge, or from the right).
  reduced: fades only (no scale, slide, zoom or geometry motion); off: nothing moves.
  Cost: flcomp composites on the CPU, there is no GLES compositing path (GLES is only for importing GPU clients), and
  the animations are drawn the same way. Nothing runs at idle: an animation exists from its start to its last frame,
  frame_done (the page flip, or a 16 ms timer in the headless backend) advances it by the clock and damages only what
  moved, which asks for the next frame; there are no timers of its own and no wakeups afterwards. A window that is not
  scaled is copied like before, a scaled one is sampled bilinearly (windows above 1.2 megapixels fade without scaling).
  Pictures (closing windows, hiding menus, the overview) are copies freed after at most about 200 ms. Under power
  saving the 30 frames a second cap applies as always. Not animated: X11 windows, anything while a fullscreen window
  is drawn alone (performance mode) or the screen is locked. FLCOMP_ANIM_LOG=1 logs frames, intervals and draw time per
  burst (anim_log=1 in desktop.conf does the same, for a session that has no environment to set); `make check-anim`
  (tests/anim.sh) tests every motion and the pacing.

Power saving (power.c, media.c, render.c; desktop.conf power_saver=1)
  The panel's battery flyout writes the setting. flcomp then draws at most 30 frames a
  second (repaints are spaced 33 ms apart) and sets the backlight to 70 % of what it was
  (desktop.conf brightness= is not touched, so a restart dims from the real value again);
  turning it off puts the old value back, unless the brightness was changed by hand
  meanwhile. The CPU governor is flpower's part (flpanel).

Performance mode (perf.c; desktop.conf performance=auto|on|off)
  auto (default): on while the window on top is fullscreen; on: always (the panel's battery
  flyout has the switch); off: never. While it is on there is no frame cap (it overrides
  power saving's 30 frames a second), and when the fullscreen window is opaque, covers the
  whole output and nothing is drawn over it (no menu, flyout, popup, notification card,
  overview, lock screen), only that window is drawn: no wallpaper fill, no windows below it,
  no panel. The Alt-Tab row, the volume indicator and the pointer still appear over it, and
  a notification or a popup brings the normal drawing back for as long as it is there. The
  log (flcomp.log) says when it switches. This is a saving of work on the CPU path, not a
  KMS scanout plane: a game's dmabuf is still read into the shadow buffer (see
  docs/E2-GAMING.md for what real direct scanout would take).

X11 programs (xwm.c, xwsel.c, xwdeco.c; recipes xwayland and the X libraries)
  flcomp is also the window manager of Xwayland, so Steam and old X11 programs run. flcomp
  listens on /tmp/.X11-unix/X<N> (first free N, mode 0700: only this user, there is no X
  authority file) from the start and exports DISPLAY=:N to every program of the session.
  Xwayland is NOT running until the first X client connects: the listening socket is handed to
  it (-listenfd), it runs rootless (-rootless), connects to flcomp over a socketpair as an
  ordinary Wayland client (the only client that may bind xwayland_shell_v1, which is hidden from
  everyone else), and gets flcomp as its window manager over a second socketpair (-wm). Three
  seconds after the last X window is gone (and XRes says no X client without a window is left)
  flcomp ends it; the next X client starts it again. Idle desktop: no Xwayland, no X memory.
  FLCOMP_NO_XWAYLAND=1 switches it all off; FLCOMP_XWAYLAND=/path names another binary (and
  switches X on in headless runs, which have none otherwise); FLCOMP_XDEBUG=1 logs what the
  window manager sees; Xwayland's own messages (and xkbcomp's) go to $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xwayland.log.
  Windows: X windows are matched to their wl_surface by WL_SURFACE_SERIAL and
  xwayland_surface_v1.set_serial. A managed X window is an ordinary struct window (xwm.c makes it
  when its surface has a buffer), so workspaces, Alt-Tab, the overview, snap and maximize,
  Super+arrows, the panel's list (app id = WM_CLASS class, lower case; title = _NET_WM_NAME or
  WM_NAME, live), General Manager and performance mode treat it like any other. Override-redirect
  windows and menus, tooltips, dock and notification types are "unmanaged": drawn above the
  managed windows where the client put them, no frame, no panel entry, no focus. Transient
  windows (WM_TRANSIENT_FOR) are centred over their parent, not in the panel. Position and
  stacking are kept in step with the X server before every repaint (the server picks the window
  under the pointer by its own stack and computes root coordinates from its own geometry);
  windows on another workspace or minimized are kept at the bottom of the X stack.
  EWMH/ICCCM: _NET_WM_STATE (fullscreen, maximized, hidden, focused, skip taskbar) both ways,
  _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW, _NET_CLOSE_WINDOW, WM_DELETE_WINDOW (else the client is killed),
  WM_TAKE_FOCUS and the input hint, _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE (a client's own title bar can move and
  resize the window), WM_NORMAL_HINTS (minimum, maximum, resize increments), _NET_FRAME_EXTENTS,
  _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE, _MOTIF_WM_HINTS (no decoration: Steam draws its own), WM_STATE,
  _NET_CLIENT_LIST, _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP, _NET_WM_DESKTOP. A window larger than the screen is
  asked to fit.
  Frames: an X window that has no title bar of its own gets one from flcomp (xwdeco.c): title in
  the built-in Plex font, minimize, maximize, close, a 3 px border and 4 px of grip outside it
  for resizing, a double click on the title maximizes. Fullscreen and maximized windows lose the
  sides; undecorated and menu windows get none. Plain palette colours, accent line when focused.
  Clipboard (xwsel.c): CLIPBOARD is bridged both ways (text as UTF8_STRING/STRING/TEXT <->
  text/plain, any target named like a mime type, INCR for large data, 64 MB at most). PRIMARY
  (select, middle click) stays among the X clients, there is no Wayland primary selection to
  connect it to. No drag and drop between X and Wayland (flcomp has none).
  Not done: X cursor images (flcomp draws its own arrow for every client), RandR resolution
  emulation for games that switch modes (needs wp_viewporter), glamor/DRI3 acceleration inside
  Xwayland (needs zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 version 4 with feedback; until then Xwayland draws in
  software and says "falling back to sw" in xwayland.log; see docs/E2-GAMING.md), a system tray,
  XSettings (DPI, themes), several X screens.

Testing
  make check-x         headless, needs Xwayland (run it in the build chroot): lazy start and stop, frames,
                       titles, focus, move, resize, maximize, fullscreen, menus, dialogs, workspaces,
                       Alt-Tab, the overview and the clipboard both ways with tests/xclient (xcb); X_OUT=DIR
                       keeps PPM screenshots (tests/ppm2png.py makes PNG of them)
  make check-perf      headless: performance mode (a fullscreen shmclient -F; part of make check)
  make check-lock      headless: the lock screen (Super+L, input, crash and restart, the hint,
                       unlock by the owner only, the lid), the notification surface and power saving
  make check           headless: FLCOMP_HEADLESS=WxH runs with no display and
                       no input devices, tests/shmclient is the test client,
                       FLCOMP_TEST_FIFO feeds fake pointer and key events.
  FLCOMP_SCREENSHOT=F  write a PPM of the screen to F on SIGUSR1 (also real).
  FLCOMP_PANEL=CMD     panel program, empty for none.
  FLCOMP_SETUP=CMD     program for --firstboot (default flsetup; the login screen
                       runs flgreet-ui this way).
  FLCOMP_SESSION=CMD   session services, default /usr/lib/flcomp/session, empty
                       for none.
  FLCOMP_TEST_FIFO lines: move X Y, button CODE 0|1, key CODE 0|1, axis STEPS, lid 0|1.
  FLCOMP_LOCK=CMD     lock program for Super+L, the lid and restarts (default fllock).
  tests/apps.py        headless tests of flfiles, fledit, flgreet-ui and flpackages (run it
                       in the build chroot; writes PNG screenshots).
  FLCOMP_KEYBOARD_CONF=FILE  instead of /etc/keyboard.conf.
  FLCOMP_BACKLIGHT=DIR       backlight directory for brightness= at start (tests).
  Headless has a made-up list of modes, so set_output can be tried without a display.
  SIGUSR2             exit with status 3 (flcrash-test uses it to test the crash screen).

Session
  After the panel, flcomp starts /usr/lib/flcomp/session as the user. It runs
  dbus-daemon --session at $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus (flcomp puts that address in
  DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS for every client), pipewire, wireplumber and
  pipewire-pulse, each only if installed, and logs to
  $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/session.log. XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is /run/user/UID, made by the
  login screen; /etc/profile.d/desktop.sh exports it. flcomp stops the script
  with SIGTERM when it exits and the script stops its daemons.
  If flatpak is installed it also adds the Flathub remote for the person once
  (flatpak --user, retried quietly while offline; stamp file
  ~/.local/share/freelfs/flathub-added).
  It also starts flupdate-notify (the update notifier), flsnap-notice (says so when the system runs
  from an earlier snapshot; exits at once otherwise) and the XDG autostart entries: every .desktop
  file in ~/.config/autostart and /etc/xdg/autostart (XDG_CONFIG_DIRS), the user's file of the same
  name winning; Hidden=true, X-GNOME-Autostart-enabled=false, X-FreeLFS-Override=true (General
  Manager's Startup tab), OnlyShowIn/NotShowIn and a missing TryExec switch an entry off.

Source map
  main.c       startup, wl_output, children, signals
  drm.c        DRM/KMS, libseat, libinput, headless backend
  render.c     damage lists, compositing, frame callbacks, screenshot
  surface.c    wl_surface, regions, subsurfaces, hit testing
  xdg.c        xdg_shell and window management
  seat.c       pointer, keyboard, key bindings, layouts, repeat, Num Lock, clipboard, cursor
  config.c     desktop.conf
  output.c     modes, scale, the confirm timer
  lock.c       the lock screen (state, restart of the lock program, the hint)
  lockfont.h   the hint's glyphs (made by tools/genlockfont.c, checked in)
  power.c      power saving: frame cap and backlight
  perf.c       performance mode: no frame cap, fullscreen window drawn alone
  xwm.c        Xwayland: the X socket, starting and ending it, xwayland_shell_v1, the X window manager
  xwsel.c      the clipboard between X and Wayland
  xwdeco.c     the frame around X windows (title bar, buttons, grip): drawing and hit testing
  xwm.h, xwm-int.h   what the rest of flcomp calls, and what the three share
  media.c      volume, brightness and microphone keys
  osd.c        the on-screen indicator
  anim.c       animations: window, workspace, overview and shell surface motion, the bilinear sampler
  theme.c      theme=, accent= and wallpaper= from desktop.conf (own inotify watch): PAL, the colour scheme; accent colour of the
               indicator, Alt-Tab frame and lock caret; wallpaper=accent|NAME|user:NAME, swapped live
  sound.c      system sounds: runs flsound (login, tick, screenshot, error); sounds=0 silences it
  flshell.c    fl_shell

Outputs and night light (round 17, output.c, nightlight.c, drm.c)
  Every connector is an output (name like HDMI-A-1, eDP-1, Virtual-1). Enabled outputs form one
  desktop: the shadow buffer is their bounding box in logical pixels (mode size * 100 / scale,
  scale 100..200 per output, any step; flcomp enlarges the picture per output, bilinear, so
  clients always draw at scale 1 and wp_fractional_scale is not needed). The primary output
  carries the panel, start menu, notifications, maximize/fullscreen/snap (C.prim); wl_output and
  the panel see the primary's logical size only. The other outputs are extra desktop space for
  windows and the pointer. Settings are saved as
    output.NAME=mode=1920x1080@60 scale=1.25 pos=0,0 enabled=1 primary=1
  in desktop.conf by flcomp (after fl_shell output_configure, i.e. `fldisplay set`), applied at
  start and when the kernel reports a hotplug (udev "drm" change, 300 ms settle). The old mode=
  and scale= keys apply to the primary until it has an output.NAME line. fl_shell 9 adds
  output_info / output_mode_info events and output_configure / output_revert requests (for
  fldisplay only; no automatic revert: the caller asks and calls `fldisplay revert`).
  Limits: panel, overview, lock text and the desktop icon layer assume the primary is at the
  top left (icons stay at 0,0); the pointer can enter empty corners between differently sized
  outputs; snapping at the screen edge uses the whole desktop's edges. FLCOMP_HEADLESS_OUTPUTS=N
  (1..4) gives N fake outputs for tests (src/fldisplay/tests/check.sh).
  Night light: night_light=0|1|auto (and nightlight=off|on|auto, which wins), night_light_temp
  (2700..6500, default 4000), night_light_from / _to (HH:MM, 20:00 and 07:00; the same with
  nightlight_ prefix). Fades in 2 s; auto is checked every 30 s. Colour factors go to the CRTC
  gamma table; outputs whose CRTC has none (virtio-gpu) get a per-pixel table in the copy to the
  screen. The log says "night light on (3000K, ...)".
